                   "the papers are public, and no paid shortcut has ever survived "
                   "contact with an NTA investigation."))
S += chapter(14, "NCERT First, Then the Right Books", c13)

# ============================================================== CH 14
c14 = []
c14.append(P("Eight months is the honest shape of a first-attempt NEET preparation: "
            "long enough to cover everything, short enough that every month has a "
            "job. Here is the runway as it should run, from this September to the "
            "Sunday in May.", lead))
c14.append(mk_table(
    ["Phase", "Months", "The job"],
    [["Foundation 1", "Sept - Oct 2026", "Class 12 chapters alongside a full Class 11 re-cover; weekly rotation of all three subjects; error log opens"],
     ["Foundation 2", "Nov - Dec 2026", "Complete first pass of the whole syllabus; 60% new content, 40% revision; chapter tests weekly"],
     ["Consolidation", "Jan 2027", "Syllabus gaps closed; full-syllabus learning ends; subject-wise tests go timed; registration window watch"],
     ["Board bridge", "Feb - Mar 2027", "Board-style written practice with 30 - 40 NEET MCQs daily; one full mock a week"],
     ["Mock season", "April 2027", "Two to three full 180-minute mocks a week, each analysed the same day; error-log raids; no new chapters"],
     ["Taper", "Exam week", "Sleep protected, light formula and diagram revision, one mock at the real 2 PM slot; nothing new"]],
    [0.20, 0.16, 0.64]))
c14.append(P("The week-by-week engine under the phases", h2))
c14.append(P("Each month above runs on the same four-part weekly cycle. It is deliberately "
             "boring — the point of a system is that you stop negotiating with it."))
c14.append(mk_table(
    ["Day", "Commitment"],
    [["Monday - Friday", "Concept blocks: new NCERT chapters per the rotation; daily numericals (Physics) and daily recall (Biology)"],
     ["Wednesday", "Chapter test on everything from the previous week — 30 minutes, timed"],
     ["Saturday", "PYQ block: previous-year questions for the week's chapters, plus one section-length timed set"],
     ["Sunday", "The review: error log updated, 30-minute week audit, next week's rotation planned — then a real half-day off"]],
    [0.22, 0.78]))
c14.append(P("If you are starting from Class 11, compress nothing — just begin "
            "Foundation 1 with the Class 11 chapters you are currently studying, "
            "and let school run parallel. If you are a dropper, Chapter 20 has your "
            "version of this calendar."))
c14.append(P("The one habit that makes this calendar work is the weekly review: "
            "every Sunday, 30 minutes, no exceptions. Which chapters did I touch this "
            "week? What did the error log teach me? What is next week's rotation? "
            "Eight months of Sundays with that habit is a rank; without it, it is "
            "eight months of activity with nothing banked."))
S += chapter(15, "The Eight-Month Runway, Month by Month", c14)
S.append(PageBreak())

# ============================================================== CH 15
c15 = []
c15.append(P("There is no perfect timetable — there is only one you will actually "
            "follow for eight months. Two templates that have survived contact with "
            "real students; adjust the clock, keep the proportions.", lead))
c15.append(P("Class 12 student (with school), about 6 - 8 hours of self-study", h2))
c15.append(mk_table(
    ["Slot", "Focus"],
    [["Early morning (60 - 90 min)", "New Physics or Chemistry concepts — freshest brain to the hardest subject"],
     ["School hours", "Attend properly; your board syllabus IS the NEET syllabus"],
     ["After school (90 min)", "Biology NCERT reading and recall of the day's chapter"],
     ["Evening session 1 (60 - 90 min)", "Physics / Physical Chemistry numericals"],
     ["Evening session 2 (60 min)", "Chapter MCQs and PYQs of everything touched today"],
     ["Before bed (30 min)", "Error log update + formula / diagram flash-recall"]],
    [0.30, 0.70]))
c15.append(P("Dropper / repeater, 8 - 10 hours", h2))
c15.append(bullets([
    "Three concept blocks (Physics, Chemistry, Biology) of 90 minutes each, morning to afternoon, one subject per block, all three every day.",
    "One 90-minute MCQ / PYQ block, alternating subjects day by day.",
    "One 60-minute revision block reserved for the error log and the week's weakest chapters.",
    "One full 180-minute mock at exam timing (2 - 5 PM) every week from January, twice a week from April.",
]))
c15.append(P("Whichever template you use, protect the two bookends: the morning "
            "hardest-subject slot and the before-bed recall. Those two slots, held "
            "for eight months, do more for a NEET score than anything in between. "
            "And sleep is not the flexible line item — seven to eight hours is part "
            "of the syllabus, because memory consolidation happens overnight."))
S += chapter(16, "A Day That Actually Works", c15)

# ============================================================== CH 17 DEEP WORK (new)
cdw = []
cdw.append(P("Eight months of content will not save eight months of bad study "
             "conditions. The difference between a 520 and a 620 is usually not "
             "knowledge — it is how many of your nominal study hours were actually "
             "spent studying.", lead))
cdw.append(P("The phone contract", h2))
cdw.append(P("Every study block you have is competing with a machine engineered to "
             "win that competition. Sign a contract with yourself instead of "
             "negotiating hourly: the phone lives in another room (not face-down on "
             "the desk — another room) during concept blocks; it comes back during "
             "meals and the evening break; notifications from every app except "
             "family contacts are off for the season. Aspirants fear this will cost "
             "them their social life; in practice it costs about ten days of "
             "fidgeting and then returns two to three usable hours a day."))
cdw.append(P("Environment beats willpower", h2))
cdw.append(bullets([
    "One desk, one purpose: study where you study, scroll where you scroll. The brain learns locations faster than it learns subjects.",
    "Two-minute rule for starting: begin with the easiest task in the block — copying a formula sheet, re-reading one summary. Starting is the hard part; momentum handles the rest.",
    "Plan the night before: a written list for tomorrow's blocks removes the morning decision-tax, which is where most blocks quietly die.",
    "One weekly half-day off, protected like a mock test. Rest that is scheduled actually restores; rest that is stolen mid-afternoon just adds guilt.",
]))
cdw.append(P("The long middle", h2))
cdw.append(P("Every NEET season has a hard stretch — usually December and February — "
             "when the excitement of starting has faded, the exam feels distant, and "
             "motivation dries up. Plan for it rather than being ambushed by it: "
             "in those weeks, drop the target from 'excellent day' to 'scheduled "
             "day'. Hit the blocks, hit the review, and let the calendar carry you. "
             "Motivation follows action far more reliably than it precedes it, and "
             "the 1 - 7 - 30 revision rhythm means even your worst fortnight keeps "
             "banking revision."))
cdw.append(P("Sleep is a subject", h2))
cdw.append(P("Memory consolidation happens during sleep — the hours you cut at "
             "night are the hours in which today's Biology actually gets filed. "
             "Seven to eight hours is the floor for a memory-dependent exam, and "
             "the exam-week rule is firmer still: a rested brain out-performs a "
             "crammed one on recall speed, and NEET is a recall-speed exam. One "
             "habit makes sleep easier: screens off thirty minutes before bed, "
             "replaced by the day's formula-sheet and exceptions-notebook recall."))
S += chapter(17, "Deep Work: Phones, Slumps and Sleep", cdw)

# ============================================================== CH 18 (mocks)
c16 = []
c16.append(P("Mocks are not a test of how much you know. They are the training "
            "ground for the only four skills that matter on exam day: reading "
            "accuracy, time allocation, negative-marking judgement, and calm. "
            "Treated that way, ten well-analysed mocks beat forty casually taken "
            "ones.", lead))
c16.append(P("The 3:1 rule", h2))
c16.append(P("For every hour you spend taking a mock, spend three hours analysing "
            "it. A 180-minute mock earns a 9-hour analysis spread over the following "
            "days. Skipping the analysis to take another mock is the single most "
            "common way serious aspirants stay stuck at the same score for months."))
c16.append(P("The error log", h2))
c16.append(P("Every wrong, guessed or slow question goes into one notebook with four "
            "columns — question / topic, what I did, why it was wrong, the fix — "
            "sorted into four buckets:"))
c16.append(bullets([
    "Didn't know: a content gap — back to that NCERT chapter the same week.",
    "Knew but calculated wrong: an execution error — practise that computation type daily for a week.",
    "Knew but ran out of time: a pacing error — your section order or question triage needs work.",
    "Misread the question: an accuracy error — slow down the first read; underline the stem's ask in every practice session.",
]))
c16.append(P("By February this notebook becomes your private question bank of "
             "everything you once got wrong — and the only revision material you "
             "need in the final fortnight."))
c16.append(P("What a page of it looks like", h2))
c16.append(mk_table(
    ["Question / topic", "What I did", "Why wrong", "Fix and date"],
    [["Bio: Human Physiology - JGA mechanism", "Marked option C", "Didn't know: never read that NCERT line", "Re-read 19.2 excretion same week (Oct 12)"],
     ["Phy: Ray Optics - lens-maker numerical", "Set up correctly, wrong sign", "Knew but calculated wrong: sign convention", "Convention drills, 5 per day for a week (Nov 3)"],
     ["Chem: Coordination - CFSE order", "Left blank in the mock", "Knew but ran out of time", "Section order fixed: inorganic flagged first (Dec 8)"]],
    [0.27, 0.18, 0.27, 0.28]))
c16.append(P("The mock ladder", h2))
c16.append(numbered([
    "October - December: chapter and unit tests, untimed or loosely timed, within a week of studying the chapter.",
    "January - February: one full 180-question mock a week, at the real 2 - 5 PM slot, OMR sheet if the paper is on paper mode practice.",
    "March: mocks continue through board season at reduced frequency — even a half-paper keeps the machinery warm.",
    "April: two to three full mocks a week, each analysed same-day; finish error-log raids of the top ten weak chapters.",
]))
S += chapter(18, "Mock Tests and the Error Log", c16)
S.append(PageBreak())

# ============================================================== CH 17
c17 = []
c17.append(P("If you are in Class 12, you are not preparing for two exams — you "
            "are preparing for one syllabus in two formats. NCERT is the shared "
            "base; Boards ask you to write it long, NEET asks you to recognise it "
            "fast. The mistake is treating February and March as a 'board break' "
            "from NEET.", lead))
c17.append(bullets([
    "Your Class 12 board syllabus is the majority of the NEET syllabus — every board hour is also a NEET hour, if you study the chapters to depth rather than to the syllabus's minimum.",
    "From November, add one board-style written answer per week per subject: the discipline of structured writing is what the 50% PCB aggregate and your board marks both need.",
    "Never drop MCQ practice during board months. Thirty to forty NEET MCQs a day — twenty minutes — keeps retrieval speed alive while you practise writing.",
    "After boards end, you get a clean run of weeks before the May exam: reserve it for full mocks and error-log revision, not for re-learning.",
]))
c17.append(P("For Class 11 students the pressure is different and lighter: use the "
            "year to make Class 11 fundamentals unshakeable, because NEET's hardest "
            "questions routinely fuse Class 11 and 12 concepts — genetics on cell "
            "biology, electrochemistry on equilibrium, modern physics on mechanics. "
            "The 2027 syllabus is close to equally split between the two years."))
c17.append(callout("THE 50 PERCENT CLAUSE",
                   "Remember the eligibility rule from Chapter 6: you need 50 percent "
                   "in PCB (40 for reserved categories) in Class 12 to convert a NEET "
                   "score into a seat. A brilliant NEET score on a weak board result "
                   "is a tragedy the rules do not forgive."))
S += chapter(19, "Boards and NEET Together", c17)

# ============================================================== CH 18
c18 = []
c18.append(P("A repeat year is not a continuation — it is a rebuild. The candidates "
            "who gain 100+ marks in a drop year are the ones who started by finding "
            "out exactly why last year's score happened, and the ones who gain "
            "nothing are those who ran the same year harder. Start with a "
            "diagnosis, not a timetable.", lead))
c18.append(numbered([
    "Autopsy your last paper and mocks: for each dropped mark, assign one of the four error buckets from Chapter 18. Patterns will emerge within an hour — most repeat-year losses are 60 percent accuracy and pacing, not knowledge.",
    "Rebuild from NCERT anyway. Droppers skip the textbook because 'already done' — and lose the direct-line Biology and Inorganic questions that decide ranks. Re-read it with fresh eyes; it is a different book the second time.",
    "Fix the exam, not just the syllabus: full 180-minute mocks weekly from October, at 2 PM, with the real section order and OMR discipline.",
    "Guard the aggregates: board percentage rules and the 50 percent PCB clause apply to your existing Class 12 result — check them once, now, before committing the year.",
]))
c18.append(P("One psychological note that droppers earn the right to hear: the field "
            "is not against you. In 2026, of the 138 candidates above 690 marks, "
            "more than 93 percent were first-timers — meaning droppers who rebuilt "
            "properly still competed at the very top. A diagnosis-first drop year "
            "with an error log is a genuine advantage, not a deficit."))
S += chapter(20, "The Dropper Track", c18)

# ============================================================== CH 19
c19 = []
c19.append(P("The 2027 form will differ in small details from the 2026 one, but the "
            "shape below is stable year to year. Treat this as the rehearsal; "
            "follow the live 2027 bulletin when it arrives.", lead))
c19.append(P("Fees in 2026 (in India; Rs. 9,500 abroad)", h2))
c19.append(mk_table(
    ["Category", "Fee"],
    [["General", "Rs. 1,700"],
     ["General - EWS / OBC - NCL", "Rs. 1,600"],
     ["SC / ST / PwBD / Third Gender", "Rs. 1,000"]],
    [0.55, 0.45]))
c19.append(P("Documents to have ready before the window opens", h2))
c19.append(bullets([
    "A recent passport photograph (the bulletin specifies size and format), a scanned signature, and a valid address proof.",
    "Class 10 certificate or birth proof for the date of birth.",
    "Category certificate (OBC - NCL / EWS / SC / ST / PwBD) in the prescribed format, issued by the competent authority.",
    "Citizenship / OCI / NRI documents where applicable.",
    "DigiLocker or Aadhaar linkage as advised in the bulletin's pre-registration notices.",
]))
c19.append(P("The ten mistakes that cost seasons", h2))
c19.append(bullets([
    "Waiting for the last day of the registration window — servers slow down and payments fail precisely then.",
    "Mistyping the name so it does not match the Class 10 certificate.",
    "Wrong category claim: OBC - NCL claimed without a valid current-year certificate.",
    "Uploading a photograph that is old, blurred, or wrongly sized — rejected at centre verification.",
    "Choosing exam cities without checking travel and stay feasibility for the city intimation that comes.",
    "Missing the correction window (in 2026: 10 - 12 March) after noticing an error.",
    "Filling PwBD columns when not eligible, or leaving them blank when eligible.",
    "Paying the fee but not submitting the form — payment alone does not register you.",
    "Not keeping the confirmation page printout.",
